Monday morning started with hundreds  gathering outside the court in Goodman  Sudan's largest city eagerly following  what was happening behind the heavily  guarded walls some demanded the death  penalty for the 41 National Security  Service officers they were charged with  the manslaughter of a metal chair a  schoolteacher in the eastern state of  Casilla in court the judge brought the  more than 12 weeks of trial to an end  the defendants are facing charges of  manslaughter which falls under article  130 which carries a death penalty the  first defendant to the 27th will all  receive the death penalty the 31st and  33rd are also sentenced to death  defendants 29 and 30 a cleared of  charges where the rest are sentenced to  jail the verdict was welcomed by a model  Harris family his torture and death in  custody provokes anger in schools all  over Sudan and a wave of protests  demanding the end of the 30-year rule of  President Omar al-bashir he was  eventually deposed in April following  months of unrest it was initially  sparked by rising food prices and grew  into a wider revolt demanding radical  change Bashir has been charged with  inciting and participating in the  killing of protesters we consider this  case to be a turning point in Sudan's  history many institutions in the past  both the judicial and Security used to  cover up the crimes committed by  security forces this is the first time  we have security forces put on trial for  torture it's the first ever we've had a  verdict on a large number of those  involved in the crime the security  forces and intelligence services used to  be a fortified Bastion that were  untouchable after what seemed like a  long wait the verdict was greeted with  celebrations since the formation of a  transitional government the demand to  bring to account those involved in  killing protesters over the past year  has been driving demonstrations for many  Monday's verdict is only the start of  the response for those calls a response  they say must include more trials of the  accused to bring justice to other  victims activists say at least 250  protesters have been killed since the  start of their revolution  12 months ago Sudan's prime minister  has set up a committee to investigate  the deaths including minal for arson  sala de madrid gun islam we welcome the  verdict today but we're also waiting for  the trials for those who killed my son  we filed a complaint and we've brought  forward witnesses but we haven't heard  of any trial yet we'll be following that  up until we bring justice for my son and  others who were killed  we will die fighting for that justice  Sudanese are celebrating what they say  is the first victory and are vowing to  continue to safeguard their revolution  until all crimes are accounted for Heba  Morgan al-jazeera Omdurman
 

